          "literatureReview": "Electrolyte testing is essential for evaluating fluid balance, acid-base status, and organ function. Modern automated analyzers use ion-selective electrode (ISE) technology for direct potentiometric measurement of electrolytes. Sodium is the primary extracellular cation, potassium the primary intracellular cation. Chloride and bicarbonate are key anions. The anion gap (Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-)) helps identify metabolic acidosis causes. Electrolyte imbalances occur in dehydration, kidney disease, endocrine disorders, gastrointestinal losses, and medication effects. Critical values require immediate attention: sodium <120 or >160 mmol/L, potassium <2.5 or >6.5 mmol/L. Hemolysis can falsely elevate potassium, so proper sample handling is crucial. Results should be interpreted with clinical context, considering symptoms, medications, and other laboratory findings."
